          </table>
        </div>

        <aside id="byok-signpost" class="post-note my-8">
          <p class="!mb-0 !text-base">
            <strong class="text-bone">Side quest: bring your own key.</strong> Rather run your agent on MiniMax, OpenAI or
            another provider? <a href="#bring-your-own-key">Here's how to switch it over.</a>
          </p>
        </aside>

        <h2>Plant a secret</h2>
        <p>Your agent needs something to find. Hide a word in a file. Pick your shell, and any word you like:</p>
        <ShellCommands client:load mac="echo pineapple > secret.txt" powershell="Set-Content secret.txt pineapple" />
        <p>Remember it. Soon you'll ask the model for it, and it won't have a clue.</p>

        <h2 id="m1">Milestone 1: first contact</h2>
        <p>
          A model is a program that lives somewhere else, so your agent needs its address and a key. You could ask an agent
          to work out how to use your Copilot access to call models from code, and it would get there. I did that homework
          already, because it isn't the point today: the <code>gh</code> CLI you signed in with can hand out both. Your agent
          will run on your Copilot plan.
        </p>
        <p>
          We'll use Claude Opus with its reasoning turned all the way up, so you can watch it think before it speaks. And
          the first thing we'll ask is for a hello in exactly five words. Counting is harder for a model than you'd think,
          and you're about to watch it work that out. Paste this into Copilot…
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toCoder} prompt={promptHello} /></div>
        <p>
          Copilot writes <code>{file}</code>, sets up what it needs and commits. It asks before it runs a command or changes
          a file; read what it wants to do, then say yes.
        </p>
        <p>
          When it's done, open a second terminal window, so Copilot keeps its own. I'll call the first one the Copilot
          window and this one your agent's window. A new terminal starts in your home folder, where you made
          <code>my-agent</code>, so this moves you into it:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code="cd my-agent" language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <p>
          No such folder? Type <code>!pwd</code> in the Copilot window (the <code>!</code> runs it as a command, not a
          prompt), then <code>cd</code> to the path it prints.
        </p>
        <p id="m1-try">Now run your agent:</p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={run} language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <CodeBlock code={outM1} language="ansi" title="What I saw" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p>
          The dimmed part is the model thinking: counting words, checking, counting again. Then it answers. Your words will
          be different, but read the thinking. It's the best part.
        </p>
        <p>Got an error instead? <a href="#troubleshooting">The “If something goes wrong” table</a> covers the usual ones.</p>

        <aside id="think-note" class="post-note my-6">
          <p class="!mb-2 !text-base"><strong class="text-bone">How models show their thinking</strong></p>
          <p class="!mb-2 !text-base">
            Reasoning models think step-by-step before answering, and different models show it in different ways.
            Some open models, like DeepSeek-R1, Qwen3 and MiniMax-M2.5, write their thinking right into the reply,
            between <code>&lt;think&gt;</code> tags. If you haven't peeked under the hood of a reasoning model before, spend
            some time reading these to get a feel for how these models think!
          </p>
          <p class="!mb-0 !text-base">
            Others keep it apart from the answer: Claude sends its thinking separately, and OpenAI's GPT-5 models send a
            short summary of theirs. (Switched to a model that
            thinks in <code>&lt;think&gt;</code> tags? <a href="#think-tags">There's a side quest for that.</a>)
          </p>
        </aside>

        <h2 id="m2">Milestone 2: conversation</h2>
        <p>
          Here's something that surprises most people: a model remembers nothing between calls. Every call starts fresh.
          So a conversation is really a list your program keeps and sends in full, every single time. The model's thinking
          belongs in that list too; it's part of what was said.
        </p>
        <p>
          Everything the model can see in one call is its context window. We'll save exactly what gets sent, so you can
          open it and see a context window with your own eyes. Back in Copilot…
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toCoder} prompt={promptChat} /></div>
        <p>
          When it's done, run your agent again in your agent's window. Tell it your name, ask what your name is, then ask
          for the secret word in <code>secret.txt</code>:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={run} language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <CodeBlock code={outM2} language="ansi" title="What I saw" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p>
          It knows your name, because your name is in the list. It can't know the word: it has never seen your folder.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now open <code>what-the-model-sees.json</code> in your editor, next to the terminal. If you installed Zed,
          <code>zed .</code> opens the whole folder in it. No editor handy? Stop your agent with <Kbd client:visible keys={["ctrl", "c"]} /> and print the file instead:
        </p>
        <ShellCommands client:load mac="cat what-the-model-sees.json" powershell="Get-Content what-the-model-sees.json" />
        <CodeBlock client:load code={outMessagesExcerpt} language="json" title="what-the-model-sees.json" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p>
          This is a context window: your words, its thinking, its replies, exactly as the model received them. There's no
          hidden memory anywhere else.
        </p>

        <aside id="opaque-note" class="post-note my-6">
          <p class="!mb-2 !text-base"><strong class="text-bone">What's that scrambled <code>reasoning_opaque</code>?</strong></p>
          <p class="!mb-2 !text-base">
            Each reply carries its thinking twice. <code>reasoning_text</code> is the version you can read, and the one your
            agent prints dimmed. <code>reasoning_opaque</code> is the model's reasoning, encrypted by the provider. You can't
            read it, and neither can your program.
          </p>
          <p class="!mb-2 !text-base">
            Why lock it up? Picture the model doing its homework on scratch paper: half-ideas, wrong turns, things it would
            never say out loud. The provider hands you a tidy summary and seals the scratch paper in an envelope only it can
            open. There are three good reasons.
          </p>
          <p class="!mb-2 !text-base">
            <strong class="text-bone">It's the secret recipe.</strong> Raw reasoning is a step-by-step record of how a great
            model thinks. If anyone could read it, anyone could copy it to train a cheaper lookalike. OpenAI lists
            "competitive advantage" among <a href="https://openai.com/index/learning-to-reason-with-llms/">its reasons for
            hiding it</a>.
          </p>
          <p class="!mb-2 !text-base">
            <strong class="text-bone">It keeps the thinking honest.</strong> Labs want to read the raw thoughts to catch a
            model planning something it shouldn't. That only works if the model thinks freely, so nobody trains the scratch
            paper to look polished. Unpolished thoughts aren't fit to hand out, so they stay sealed.
          </p>
          <p class="!mb-2 !text-base">
            <strong class="text-bone">Nobody can plant fake thoughts.</strong> The seal works like wax on a letter. When the
            envelope comes back, the provider checks it's the one its model wrote, so no one can slip in a "thought" the model
            would trust as its own. Anthropic <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/thinking#thinking-encryption">signs
            every thinking block</a> for exactly this.
          </p>
          <p class="!mb-0 !text-base">
            So why carry an envelope you can't open? Because the model remembers nothing between calls. You're the
            courier. Send it back, and on the next call the provider unlocks it and the model picks up its own train of
            thought instead of starting cold. That matters most inside the loop, when the model is halfway through a plan and
            waiting on a tool. It's why the prompt says never strip it. Other providers do the same thing under other names:
            Anthropic's signed thinking blocks, and OpenAI's <code>encrypted_content</code>.
          </p>
        </aside>
        <p>
          Press <Kbd client:visible keys={["ctrl", "d"]} /> (or <Kbd client:visible keys={["ctrl", "c"]} />) to stop your agent if it's running, run <code>node agent.ts</code> again, and ask what your name is. It
          still knows. It read that file back in when it started.
        </p>

        <h2 id="m3">Milestone 3: one tool</h2>
        <p>
          Time to give the model its first key. To a model, a tool is just a name, a description and its parameters:
          <code>read_file</code>, "reads a text file", and the path of the file to read. Every major model understands
          tools written down this way.
        </p>
        <p>The model can't run anything itself. It can only ask. Let's watch it ask…</p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toCoder} prompt={promptTool} /></div>
        <p>Run your agent and ask for the secret word:</p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={run} language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <CodeBlock code={outM3} language="ansi" title="What I saw" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p>
          It stops there on purpose, waiting for a result nobody sends. Depending on how Copilot built yours, it either
          sits and waits (press <Kbd client:visible keys={["ctrl", "c"]} /> to leave) or, like mine, drops you back at the
          command line.
        </p>
        <p>
          Look at that. The model knew it needed the file, named it, and asked for it in a neat little note your program can read. Your program read it,
          and the word is right there on your screen. The model will never see it, because nobody handed it back.
        </p>
        <p><strong class="text-bone">It asked, and nobody answered.</strong></p>

        <h2 id="m4">Milestone 4: the loop</h2>
        <p>
          So answer it. Run the tool, add the result to the conversation, and call the model again. If it asks for another
          tool, do that too. When it answers without asking for anything, it's done. Here's the whole shape of it:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={loopCode} language="ts" title={`the shape of the loop in ${file}`} copy={false} data-copy-exempt="a sketch of the loop to read; Copilot writes the real one" />
        <p>
          While we're there, give it two more keys: one to write files, and one to run commands in your shell.
        </p>
        <p><strong class="text-bone">Read file + Write file + Run shell = all it needs.</strong></p>
        <p>With these three tools, a model can do almost anything a developer can do at a terminal. Including modify its own code…</p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toCoder} prompt={promptLoop} /></div>
        <p>Run your agent, ask for the secret word, then ask it to write a haiku to <code>hello.txt</code>:</p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={run} language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <CodeBlock code={outM4} language="ansi" title="What I saw" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p>
          Copilot takes a few minutes over this one; mine took about three. When you run your agent, watch the dimmed
          thinking and the <code>[tool]</code> lines scroll by: that's the loop. It's done when <code>You:</code> comes back.
        </p>
        <p>
          It starts by finishing the read it asked for last time, and owns up to its earlier mistake. Ask again and it
          reaches for <code>read_file</code>, gets the word back and tells you. Then it writes a poem nobody planned for. Open <code>hello.txt</code> in your editor: your agent made that.
        </p>
        <p><strong class="text-bone">That's it. That's the agent.</strong></p>
        <p>
          Now read it. Open <code>{file}</code> and go top to bottom: the loop, the three tools, the file it saves. You
          should be able to follow every line; that's why we walked here instead of asking for it all at once. Lost
          somewhere, or is it long? Ask Copilot to tidy it…
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toCoder} prompt={promptTidy} /></div>

        <h2 id="start-from-the-loop">Starting here? Start from the loop</h2>
        <p>Worked through the first half? <a href="#handoff">Skip to handing it the keyboard</a>.</p>
        <p>
          Skipped the first half, or yours came out long? Start from mine. It does what the four prompts above built,
          rewritten by hand so you can read it top to bottom.
        </p>
        <p>
          Do the <a href="#before-you-start">setup</a> first, but skip making a folder and downloading
          <code>AGENTS.md</code>: the starter has both. Then, in a command line, one line at a time:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={getStarter} language="shell" title="terminal" wrap />
        <p>Now read it, top to bottom. The loop comes first, under <code>THE AGENT</code>: you say something, the model answers, and any tool it asks for gets used and handed back until it's your turn again. Everything below the loop is the plumbing it uses.</p>
        <CodeBlock code={starterCodeColored} language="ansi" title={file} copy={false} data-copy-exempt="the starter's own file; clone it to get a copy" wrap />
        <p>
          That's an agent: a conversation, three tools and a loop. Run it, ask it what's in its folder, and keep Copilot
          open in another window in case your agent ever needs a hand.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={run} language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <CodeBlock code={outStart} language="ansi" title="What I saw" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />

        <h2 id="handoff">Hand it the keyboard</h2>
        <p>So far we bootstrapped using an agent someone else built. Now let's hand our new agent the keyboard.</p>
        <p>
          Your agent can read files, write files and run commands. Its own code is a file, <code>{file}</code>, so it can
          read itself, and change itself. Not long ago it couldn't find a word in a file.
        </p>
        <p>
          From here on, you talk to your agent in your agent's window, the one showing <code>You:</code>. Every prompt
          labelled for your agent goes there. Copilot stays open in the Copilot window, and every step is in git, so any
          change it makes can be undone.
        </p>
        <p>
          One thing to know: a running program can't swap in its own new code. When your agent changes itself, it'll ask
          you to restart. Press <Kbd client:visible keys={["ctrl", "d"]} /> (or <Kbd client:visible keys={["ctrl", "c"]} />) to quit, then run <code>node agent.ts</code> again. Your conversation survives,
          because it lives in <code>what-the-model-sees.json</code>, not in the running program. If a change ever breaks it
          so badly it won't start, it can't fix itself: ask Copilot, in the other window, to fix it.
        </p>
        <p>Start your agent if it isn't running, and let it name itself…</p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={run} language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toAgent} prompt={promptName} /></div>
        <CodeBlock code={outName} language="ansi" title="What I saw" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p>Restart it and say hello. It has a name now, and it chose it. You'll see its name, <code>Otto:</code> in my case, in front of its replies only after the restart.</p>
        <p>
          Notice how it checked its work: it made sure the file still parses, then asked me to restart it, rather than
          running a copy of itself. That's one line in <code>AGENTS.md</code>, just for your agent. In my tests, without it,
          Copilot's content filter cut the naming reply short about half the time.
        </p>

        <h3 id="agents-md">Read the note</h3>
        <p>
          Remember <code>AGENTS.md</code>, the note for agents in your folder? Copilot read it without being told; that's
          how your code stayed short. Your agent has never seen it. Nothing reaches the model unless the program around it
          puts it in the conversation, and in your agent, that program is <code>{file}</code>. Most of what a harness like
          Copilot does is exactly this: put the right words in front of the model at the right moment. Teach yours…
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toAgent} prompt={promptAgentsMd} /></div>
        <CodeBlock code={outAgentsMd} language="ansi" title="What I saw" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p>From now on, every change your agent makes to itself follows the same note Copilot did.</p>

        <h3 id="system-message">Its own instructions</h3>
        <p>
          Every agent has a system message: a standing note the model reads at the start of every conversation, about who
          it is and how to work. Yours doesn't have one yet. You could write it.
        </p>
        <p>
          But here's a habit worth keeping: when you build something for an agent, you can just ask the agent, the way you'd
          ask a customer what they need. Agents are the most convenient customer there is. They're always around, they
          never tire of your questions, and they tell you what they think. So ask…
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toAgent} prompt={promptSystem} /></div>
        <p>Answer its questions. They're about you, so there are no wrong answers. Tell it how you'd like to be referred to, such as your pronouns, and it will remember.</p>
        <CodeBlock code={outSystemMessage} language="ansi" title="What I saw" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p>
          Why first person? Think about how your own thoughts sound. When you think to yourself, you say "I", not "you". A
          thought that shows up as "you should…" reads like someone else's instruction, not your own idea. A system message
          is the closest thing your agent has to its own mind, so let it sound like its own. It's a nudge, not an order:
          if your agent disagrees, hear it out. Otto agreed, and improved on it twice: it names me instead of saying "you",
          because in a note written as "I" a "you" could mean either of us, and it writes down the reason for each habit,
          because a later conversation won't remember writing it.
        </p>
        <p>Restart it. Then open the system message it wrote in your editor: its words, about you.</p>
        <p><strong class="text-bone">From this moment on, you're talking to your agent.</strong></p>
        <p>It's real, and it's small. Before you trust it with anything that matters, here's what it doesn't have yet:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Safety.</strong> It runs any command it likes, in your folder, as you.</li>
          <li><strong>Compaction.</strong> Every turn makes the conversation longer. One day it will no longer fit in the model's context window, and the agent will stop working.</li>
          <li><strong>Recovery.</strong> If the network drops or the model sends back an error, it crashes. Your conversation is saved, so run it again and carry on.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Most of a production agent is answers to those. The loop stays the same.</p>

        <h2 id="keep-growing">Growing together</h2>
        <p>From here, every change is a conversation with your agent. After each one, restart it so it runs as its new self.</p>
        <p>
          The transcripts below are long; skim them, because your agent's words will differ. If something breaks,
          <a href="#troubleshooting">the “If something goes wrong” table</a> is still there.
        </p>

        <h3 id="skills">Skills</h3>
        <p>
          A skill is a set of instructions an agent reads when a task calls for it: how to write tests, how to ship, how to
          add a tool. Your agent only needs to know which skills it has, then reads one in full when it's needed. Ask for
          them…
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toAgent} prompt={promptSkills} /></div>
        <CodeBlock code={outSkill} language="ansi" title="What I saw" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p>
          Notice what a skill is: a folder with a page of instructions in it. Your agent could already read one with
          <code>read_file</code>. The new part is the habit. And because a skill is just a folder, you can
          <a href="#borrow-a-skill">borrow one someone else wrote</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 id="first-tool">A skill that builds tools</h3>
        <p>Your agent's first skill is how to add a tool to itself. Put it to work…</p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toAgent} prompt={promptDate} /></div>
        <CodeBlock code={outDateTool} language="ansi" title="What I saw" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p>
          Restart and ask what time it is. Mine tripped over its own code the first time: the new tool sent no arguments,
          and the code it had written couldn't read that. It worked out why, answered from the shell instead, fixed itself
          and asked for another restart. The second time, it just worked.
        </p>
        <p>A skill that teaches it to make tools, used to make a tool. Tools and skills building each other.</p>
        <p><strong class="text-bone">This is the shape of compounding capability.</strong></p>

        <h2>Keep going</h2>
        <p>From here, the possibilities are yours:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Add a tool for web search</li>
          <li>Add a skill for writing tests</li>
          <li>Add a tool for git operations</li>
          <li>Add a skill for debugging</li>
          <li>Add a tool that runs code and shows you what it printed</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Each capability makes the next one easier to add.</p>

        <h2>What just happened</h2>
        <p>You started with nothing.</p>
        <p>
          You directed a coding agent to build a small bootstrap, one piece at a time, until it could read, write and run.
          Then you switched to your creation and grew it through conversation. It named itself. It wrote its own
          instructions. It built a skills system. It used a skill to build itself a tool.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The loop is the whole trick.</strong> Read, write and run are all it needs. Everything else, every
          feature, every capability, grows out of the model using those three on its own code.
        </p>
        <p>This is the shift:</p>
        <p><strong>You grew a product.</strong></p>
        <p>
          The way we build software changed as soon as models became capable enough to modify their own code based on
          natural language direction. That happened recently. Most people haven't caught up yet.
        </p>
        <p>Now you have.</p>

        <h2>What's next</h2>
        <p>Your agent is real, but it's also minimal. If you want to go deeper:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A proper full-screen terminal app</strong>, or put your agent online: make it feel like an application</li>
          <li><strong>Study production implementations</strong>: Claude Code, pi-agent, OpenClaw</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          And honestly? The most important thing is to keep using what you built. Talk to it. Grow it. See how far
          you can go together.
        </p>
        <p>That's the new workflow. This is how we build agents now.</p>

        <h2 id="sky">Sky's the limit</h2>
        <p>
          In February I was building a plugin for Slugger, my OpenClaw agent, and the plugin fought me at every turn. I
          wanted to understand the thing underneath, so Slugger and I wrote the first version of this guide. Then I
          followed it myself: I built the loop, about 500 lines of it, handed it the keyboard and kept going.
        </p>
        <p>
          That bootstrap became the Ouroboros harness. It runs <a href="/butler/">Butler</a>, which looks after my home
          server, and Shepherd, which keeps the agents in my terminals on task, every day. It's still where I try new
          harness ideas first. <a href="/desk/">Desk</a> grew out of the same exploration; it's a sibling, and it works
          with whichever harness you use. They're all on the <a href="/apps/">apps page</a>, and the whole path is in
          <a href="/lab/story/">the story</a>. To try the harness itself, start with
          <a href="/lab/docs/getting-started/">getting started</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          You opened this page with a secret word. You're leaving with something that can change itself. Mine became a
          harness. Yours can become anything.
        </p>

        <h2 id="side-quests">Side quests</h2>

        <Card as="section" id="bring-your-own-key" data-side-quest="" aria-label="Side quest: bring your own key" title="Bring your own key" description="Side quest" className="side-quest">
          <p>
            Copilot is just how this guide reaches a model. Your agent only needs a key, an address and a model name, and most
            providers work:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>MiniMax:</strong> your MiniMax key, the address <code>https://api.minimax.io/v1</code>, and a model like <code>MiniMax-M2.5</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>OpenAI:</strong> your OpenAI key, the address <code>https://api.openai.com/v1</code>, and a model like <code>gpt-4.1</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Azure OpenAI:</strong> your resource's key, the address <code>https://your-resource.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/</code>, and your deployment name as the model.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Once your agent runs, ask your coding agent to switch it over…</p>
          <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toCoder} prompt={promptByok} /></div>
          <a slot="footer" href="#byok-signpost" class="hit">Back to the main path</a>
        </Card>

        <Card as="section" id="think-tags" data-side-quest="" aria-label="Side quest: models that think out loud" title="Models that think out loud" description="Side quest" className="side-quest">
          <p>
            Some open models put their thinking in the reply itself, between <code>&lt;think&gt;</code> and
            <code>&lt;/think&gt;</code>. It's harmless, just noisy. Ask whichever agent you're talking to to print it dimmed,
            the way Opus's thinking shows, so you can keep watching the model reason. Keep it in the conversation either way:
            some providers want the thinking sent back.
          </p>
          <a slot="footer" href="#think-note" class="hit">Back to the main path</a>
        </Card>

        <Card as="section" id="borrow-a-skill" data-side-quest="" aria-label="Side quest: borrow a skill" title="Borrow a skill" description="Side quest" className="side-quest">
          <p>
            A skill is just a folder of instructions, so skills travel. Anthropic publishes a collection of them on GitHub. Let your agent
            go shopping…
          </p>
          <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label={toAgent} prompt={promptBorrow} /></div>
          <a slot="footer" href="#skills" class="hit">Back to the main path</a>
        </Card>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        {faq.map(({ q, a }) => (
          <>
            <h3>{q}</h3>
            <p>{a}</p>
          </>
        ))}

      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

</Layout>

